  padding: 0 0.75rem;
}

.site-footer {
  padding: 1rem;
  background-color: #f3f5f8;
}
`
			},
			checks: [
				{
					id: 'plan-section',
					kind: 'exists',
					selector: '#auth-plan'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-blanks',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#auth-plan',
					matches: '^[^…]*$'
				},
				{
					id: 'need-answer',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#need',
					matches: '[Nn]ot\\s+(?:needed|required)|[Nn]eeded|[Rr]equired|[Nn]o\\s+need'
				},
				{
					id: 'need-reason-action',
					kind: 'field',
					selector: '#need',
					field: 'mainAction.label'
				},
				{
					id: 'method',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#method',
					matches: 'password|Google|Apple|GitHub|OAuth|passkey|SMS|link|no sign-in|no login'
				},
				{
					id: 'three-roles',
					kind: 'count',
					selector: '#auth-plan tr.role',
					min: 3
				},
				{
					id: 'visitor-owner',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#auth-plan tr.role',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*[Vv]isitor)'
				},
				{
					id: 'owner-role',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#auth-plan',
					matches: '[Oo]wner|[Aa]dmin'
				},
				{
					id: 'roles-can',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#auth-plan td.can',
					matches: '\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+'
				},
				{
					id: 'password-scheme',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#passwords',
					matches: 'hash|bcrypt|argon2|scrypt|PBKDF2|passwordless|no password|passkey|link'
				},
				{
					id: 'pd-note',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#pd',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:GDPR|[Pp]ersonal data))(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:consent|privacy|lawful))'
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each term with the question it answers',
				pairs: [
					['authentication', 'who are you? (a passport at the gate)'],
					['authorization', 'what may you do? (a pass that opens certain doors)'],
					['session', 'how the server remembers you between requests after you sign in'],
					[
						'password hash',
						'an irreversible “checksum” of the password, stored instead of the password itself'
					]
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'How should a server store users’ passwords?',
				options: [
					'Only as a slow hash with a salt (argon2id, bcrypt): the password itself can’t be recovered',
					'In plain text, so a forgotten one can be sent back',
					'Encrypted with one shared key'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. If the database is stolen, the passwords can’t be read from the hashes (and the salt stops anyone attacking all of them at once). A slow algorithm makes guessing expensive.',
					'No: that leaks every password at once, and people use one password on many sites. This is exactly why a site can’t “remind” you of a password: it doesn’t know it, it can only reset it.',
					'No: a shared key stolen with the database reveals all the passwords. A hash is irreversible, encryption is not.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'Which flags protect a session cookie?',
				options: [
					'HttpOnly: the page’s scripts can’t read the cookie',
					'Secure: the cookie is sent only over HTTPS',
					'SameSite: limits sending the cookie from other sites',
					'Public: every visitor can see the cookie'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: if a foreign script gets into the page (XSS), it can’t steal the session.',
					'Yes: over plain HTTP the cookie could be intercepted.',
					'Yes: it protects against forged requests from other sites (CSRF).',
					'No: there is no such flag, and the point of the protection is the opposite.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What is true of a JWT (a token that looks like xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz)?',
				options: [
					'It is signed but not encrypted: anyone can read the contents, but without the secret nobody can forge it',
					'It is encrypted, and only the server can read it',
					'It stays valid forever'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. The first two parts are just base64 of JSON, and anybody can decode them. The signature (the third part) protects against tampering. So no secrets go in a JWT, and its lifetime is kept short.',
					'No: encryption and signing are different things. An encrypted token is called a JWE and is rarer.',
					'No: a token has an expiry (the exp field), and revoking it early is hard, which is why the lifetime is short.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What happens when someone signs in with Google (OAuth / OpenID Connect)?',
				options: [
					'The password is typed only on Google’s page, and your site gets confirmation of who the person is (and, with permission, some data)',
					'Your site gets the password from Google and checks it itself',
					'Google creates the user in your database'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Your site never sees the password and trusts the provider, who confirmed that the person signed in. You don’t store passwords, but the user’s data passes through a third-party operator, and the privacy notice has to say so.',
					'No: that is the whole point: the password never leaves the provider’s page.',
					'No: you create the user in your own database yourself, once you have the confirmation.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Why does a passkey resist phishing (a fake site)?',
				options: [
					'The private key stays on the device, and the signature is tied to the address of the real site: on a fake one, sign-in simply won’t work',
					'Because a passkey is longer than any password',
					'Because the server stores the passkey'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. At registration a pair of keys is made: the private one never leaves the device, and the site keeps the public one. The browser gives a signature only to the address the key was made for.',
					'No: length isn’t the point. A password of any length can be coaxed out of someone and typed into a fake page.',
					'No: the server stores only the public half, and the private half is yours.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'In an interface, the “Delete inquiry” button is hidden from visitors. Is that enough to stop them deleting an inquiry?',
				options: [
					'No: rights must be checked on the server, because the request can be sent without the button',
					'Yes: what can’t be seen doesn’t exist',
					'Yes, if the button is hidden with the style display: none'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A hidden button is a convenience, not protection: anyone can send the same request from the console or from a program. Authorization is checked on the server on every request.',
					'No: what is hidden is still in the page and in the API.',
					'No: display: none hides things on the screen only.'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'server-authn-authz',
				front: 'How does authentication differ from authorization?',
				back: 'Authentication answers “who are you?” (signing in). Authorization answers “what may you do?” (rights and roles). The first is checked once, at sign-in, the second on every request, and on the server.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-session-cookie',
				front: 'How does signing in with a session and a cookie work?',
				back: 'After sign-in the server creates a session and gives the browser a cookie with its number (Set-Cookie). The browser sends it with every request. The flags: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-password-hash',
				front: 'How should passwords be stored?',
				back: 'Never as plain text: only as a slow hash with a salt (argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt). Limit the number of attempts, check against lists of leaked passwords, add a second factor where possible, or move to passwordless sign-in.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-jwt-oauth',
				front: 'What are JWT and OAuth?',
				back: 'JWT: a signed (not encrypted!) token of three parts that anyone can read. OAuth 2.0: a protocol for delegating access; OpenID Connect on top of it gives sign-in through a third-party provider (Google, Apple, GitHub).'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-passkeys',
				front: 'What are passkeys?',
				back: 'Passwordless sign-in under the WebAuthn standard: a pair of keys, the private key stays on the device, and a fingerprint, face or PIN confirms it. Resistant to phishing, and the server holds nothing worth stealing.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{
				title: 'MDN: HTTP authentication',
				url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Authentication',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'OWASP Cheat Sheet: Authentication',
				url: 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'OWASP Cheat Sheet: Session Management',
				url: 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'OAuth 2.0: description and specifications',
				url: 'https://oauth.net/2/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'passkeys.dev: passkeys for developers',
				url: 'https://passkeys.dev/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'ICO: Guide to the UK GDPR',
				url: 'https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Yandex ID: documentation for developers',
				url: 'https://yandex.ru/dev/id/doc/ru/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'ru'
			}
		],
		videos: [
			{
				title: 'Session vs Token Authentication in 100 Seconds (Fireship)',
				url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UBUNrFtufWo',
				licence: 'proprietary-link',
				kind: 'video',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		guide: {
			intro: 'The main decision of this lesson may well be “no sign-in”. Think about your project’s main action and your visitors, and decide honestly. And if sign-in is needed, think through the roles and how passwords are stored.',
			hints: [
				'In “Is sign-in needed”, say “not needed” or “needed” and give the reason: what the visitor does on the site (the main action) and whether they need an account for it. Name the action in the words of your brief.',
