## Authorization: roles and rights

Once it is clear *who* has come, the question is what they may do. The simplest and most common method is **roles**. Each user is given a role (visitor, editor, owner), and each role a set of rights (read, change texts, delete, see inquiries). Hand out as little as possible: **the minimum needed for the work**.

There is one main rule of authorization: **check on the server, on every request**. Hiding the “Delete” button from visitors is convenient, but it isn’t protection: a request can be sent without the button, from the console or from a program. If the server doesn’t check rights, the button protects nothing.

## Privacy law and sign-in

An email address and a name are personal data. In the EU the GDPR applies to them, and in the United Kingdom the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018. In short: you need a **lawful basis** for processing (consent is one; a contract or a legitimate interest can be others), a **privacy notice** that says what you collect and why, and a contract with each service that processes the data for you. When a provider such as Google or Apple takes part in signing in, say so in the notice, and if the provider is outside the UK or the EEA, the rules on international transfers apply. Several US states have comparable laws, such as California’s CCPA. For comparison, Russia’s law (152-FZ) also asks for consent and a policy, and in addition requires the first recording of Russian citizens’ data to be made on servers in Russia.

This is general information, not legal advice. In the UK the Information Commissioner’s Office (ico.org.uk) explains the rules, and in the EU you can ask your national data protection authority; the European Data Protection Board (edpb.europa.eu) publishes shared guidance.

## Practice

You are on a site from the 2022 era. Before you think about how to build sign-in, decide whether it is needed. Ask yourself: what does a visitor do on my site (the main action from the brief), and must they be recognized to do it? For an inquiry through a form, most often not.

If sign-in is needed even for the owner alone, describe three roles and what each may do, choose a way to sign in, and say how passwords are stored (or that there are none). And be sure to mention the law: an email address and a name are personal data (the GDPR), and signing in with Google or by SMS is processing of that data with third-party operators taking part. A lawful basis and a privacy notice are needed.

## What was wrong before

Passwords were stored as plain text, then as an unsalted MD5 hash (“enough that it can’t be read”), and at every leak millions of passwords were cracked within hours. “Secret questions” (“your mother’s maiden name”) were guessed or found on social networks. The session number was put right in the address of the page (`?sid=...`), and a forwarded link handed over somebody else’s account. Basic authentication sent the login and password in the open with every request. Password rules (“eight characters, a digit, a capital and a symbol”) made people invent weak and repeated passwords.

Today’s advice is the opposite: long passwords without fussy rules, a check against lists of leaked ones, a slow hash with a salt, a second factor, and better still passkeys and signing in through a provider. The best solution is not to store passwords at all.
